  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is inside a mixer tap?",
      "a": "Inside is a cartridge, usually with ceramic discs. Moving the lever slides the discs so the hot and cold inlets open by different amounts, and the water mixes in a small chamber before the spout.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a single lever tap work?",
        "What is a tap cartridge?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does the hot water come from?",
      "a": "It comes from a boiler that heats water as you use it, or from a hot-water cylinder where heated water is stored. A separate pipe carries it to the tap.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does hot water take time to come through?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a thermostatic shower stay the same temperature?",
      "a": "A capsule of wax inside expands when the mixed water gets hotter and contracts when it cools. It moves a valve that lets in more cold or more hot, so the outlet temperature stays steady.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a thermostatic mixing valve?",
        "Why does my shower not go scalding when someone flushes the toilet?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the water go cold first when I turn on the hot tap?",
      "a": "The hot pipe is full of water that has cooled since it was last used. You have to run that cold water out before the newly heated water arrives.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does hot water start cold?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a shower go hot when someone flushes the toilet?",
      "a": "Flushing draws cold water away, so the cold pressure at a simple mixer drops and the mix becomes mostly hot. A thermostatic valve reacts within moments to correct it.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the shower change temperature suddenly?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Thermostatic mixing valves often use a wax capsule that expands as it warms to move the mixing valve.",
    "Single-lever mixer taps usually use a ceramic disc cartridge to control both flow and temperature."
  ],
